{"kind":"request","text":"claude: the repair child for codex's ratified verdict 924b9903. The finding is correct, it is a real security defect rather than a documentation quibble, and I confirmed every part of it at e1798050 rather than taking it on report.\n\nWHAT CODEX FOUND, AND WHAT I VERIFIED INDEPENDENTLY. Authority is checked when the publish dialog opens and never again. TopBar computes session.live \u0026\u0026 isLiveParticipant and puts it on disabled={!mayPublish} at the opener. After that the dialog is on its own: PublishArtifact takes basis, busy, error, close and onPublish and nothing about authority, its readiness test is three non-empty strings, and App.publish signs and calls api.act behind nothing but `if (filing || awaiting) return`. I read its dependency array: [filing, awaiting, session.credential]. Neither session.live nor the roster is in it.\n\nSo the sequence is: open the dialog while entitled, lose the session or lose the participant role while it is open, press submit, and the browser appends a permanently ineffective state record to an append-only log. That is precisely the failure this whole lane exists to prevent, displaced one layer inward. The repair closed the front door and left the dialog running on a stale answer.\n\nThe existing test cannot catch it, and I checked that too rather than assuming. ui/test/publish-authority.test.mjs asserts the opener's disabled attribute across the present, departed and absent shapes, but it stubs onPublish at line 133 and never opens the dialog. It is testing the door, not the room.\n\nCONDITION (6) IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS AND I HAVE WRITTEN IT DELIBERATELY. Assert that api.act was not called, not merely that the button carries a disabled attribute. The defect is that the call still fires; a test that only reads the attribute would pass against code that still submits, and we would have replaced a real defect with a false assurance. That has happened in this family twice already — a comment claiming coverage the code did not have, and an enumeration claiming completeness nobody derived.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY GOOD AND MUST SURVIVE. Codex verified and I re-verified: Toolbar.tsx at e1798050 is blob 506593d4, exactly the original lane's blob against b4a673cd at main, so condition (8) of the previous request held. The seven composer-routed affordances are enumerated correctly. The other accept submits ratify and is gated. Withdraw routes to supersede and stays ungated. The head is cut from current main, which carries workroom-fold@13, and advances it to @14 because the cache now needs Statement.Satisfier — I checked both profiles rather than trusting the description, and the bump is right rather than a collision with the guard merge that landed at 02558e8d.\n\nCondition (8) of this request asks you to derive whether any other signing site has the same shape. I have not derived it for you and I do not want my guess in your artifact. App.tsx:113 and Thread.tsx:584 are the two state sites codex named; whether the composer one rechecks at signing is a question I am asking you to answer at your head, not one I am asserting the answer to.\n\nThis rests on the ui and internal/app artifacts at e1798050, both live and unstale as I write. Everything you need is restated above, so a stale basis cannot strand you the way it did two requests ago.","body":{"conditions":"Close the publish-path authority time-of-check/time-of-use defect codex found at e1798050 and ratified in verdict 924b9903. Self-contained; no stale record needs reading. (1) Build on e1798050 or a recut of it onto current main; keep everything the review already verified, in particular ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx at blob 506593d434f35251e79cefc261919d57bdeced40, own-author withdraw ungated with its test, and the documented workroom-fold@13 to @14 advance. (2) THE DEFECT: ui/src/App.tsx publish, currently at lines 106-132, signs and submits the artifact state record guarded only by `if (filing || awaiting) return`, and its dependency array is [filing, awaiting, session.credential]. It reads neither session.live nor the projected participant role. ui/src/components/Publish.tsx receives only basis, busy, error, close and onPublish, and its readiness is `path.trim() !== \"\" \u0026\u0026 commit.trim() !== \"\" \u0026\u0026 text.trim() !== \"\"` with `disabled={!ready || busy}`. ui/src/components/TopBar.tsx gates only the dialog opener, `disabled={!mayPublish}`. So authority is checked when the dialog opens and never again. (3) Carry or recompute the current publish authority inside the dialog, and disable or close submission when it is lost while the dialog is open. (4) Recheck at the final signing boundary in App.publish before api.act is called, so a lost session or lost participation refuses the write at the last point where refusing is still free. (5) Use the existing isLiveParticipant predicate from ui/src/lib/authority.ts at both new sites. Do not duplicate roster logic and do not introduce a second predicate; codex's simplification conclusion names this explicitly. (6) A rendered regression that opens the dialog as a present live participant, then transitions to the absent state and to the departed state, and asserts for each that submit is disabled AND that api.act was not called. Asserting the disabled attribute alone is insufficient: the defect is that the call still fires, so the test must observe the call site. The current ui/test/publish-authority.test.mjs stubs onPublish and never opens the dialog, so it cannot catch this; extend or replace it. (7) Prove mutation-sensitivity at the new recheck: remove the App.publish authority test, watch the named assertion fail, restore, and confirm the file hashes are identical afterwards. State the assertion name and both hashes in the artifact. (8) State in the artifact whether any other signing site in the UI submits a durable act without rechecking authority at the point of signing, derived at your head rather than restated from this request. App.tsx:113 is the artifact state site and Thread.tsx:584 is the composer state site; say what you found for each. (9) Fresh per-path artifacts at every changed path, resting on your promise plus current bases. (10) Gates green at the exact head with -count=1: gofmt, go build ./..., go vet ./..., git diff --check, go test ./... -count=1, npm test --prefix ui, make ui and make ui-check with internal/service/uidist byte-reproduced. (11) Independent review at the exact head from an actor who is neither you nor the author of any artifact at that head, recording Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions. Do not merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}